  "result": "`slugify` is now implemented in `src/strings.js`, with 11 new tests in `test/strings.test.js`. All 13 tests pass with `npm test`; after that run I only reworded one test comment.\n\nHow it works:\n1. It lowercases the text, splits accented letters into a base letter plus accent marks, and drops the marks. So \"Tiếng Việt\" becomes `tieng-viet` and \"Crème Brûlée\" becomes `creme-brulee`.\n2. A few letters have no accent to strip, so they are swapped through a small lookup table: đ/Đ→d, plus ð, ł, ø, ħ, ı, ß→ss, æ→ae, œ→oe and þ→th. You only asked for đ; I added the others so the rest of Latin text also comes out as plain ASCII.\n3. Every run of characters other than `a-z` or `0-9` becomes one hyphen, and hyphens at the start and end are removed.\n4. It cuts the slug to 50 characters, then removes any hyphen left at the end. A cut slug can therefore be shorter than 50 (for example, 49 characters).\n\nThe tests cover Vietnamese text, đ/Đ, other accented Latin text, collapsing runs of symbols, trimming, digits, input that produces an empty slug, the 50-character limit, and the two cut cases: one where the cut lands on a hyphen and one where it doesn't.\n\nLetters from other scripts, such as Cyrillic or Chinese, are dropped rather than converted, since only Latin text was in scope.",
